  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNFPA Supplies Phase III 2021-2023</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA) launched UNFPA Supplies in 2007 - a multi-donor thematic trust fund managed by UNFPA - to support country-driven actions to: ensure a secure, steady and reliable supply of quality reproductive health commodities - contraceptives, male and female condoms, and maternal health medicines -; improve their access and use by strengthening national health systems and services; and ensure Family Planning is afforded a higher level of priority in national strategies, programmes, plans and budgets. The UNFPA Supplies programme is supporting actions in 56 low income countries in Asia and the Pacific, Middle East, Latin America/Caribbean and Africa. The Programme has demonstrated capacity to deliver results, and is a key partner in major global initiatives addressing reproductive and maternal health. This is the third phase of the partnership and changes build on mid-term evaluation from preceeding phase. The new phase takes a stronger country focus by pushing domestic resource mobilisation in an effort to improve sustainability. Norway has supported UNFPA also in 2014-2015, and 2018-2020.

Expected outcome targets include: a contribution of one third of all new contraceptive users reached within the FP2020 initiative as well as a 1.25 percentage point annual increase in the modern contraceptive prevalence rate (mCPR) among all women &#x2013; including young women and those in the lowest wealth quintiles - in the 46 target countries. The Programme aims to strengthen national capacity and systems, including in-country supply chains.</narrative>
  </description>
